Scott & Krista: And then…

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:23.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:28.000
Scott & Krista: Uh, no, I'm not really sure. I don't know… I don't know enough to know what the best…

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:27.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah. Mm-hmm.

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:28.000
Whitney Hodges: Mmhm.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:34.000
Scott & Krista: sequence or combination of those things is, but I think having them.

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:31.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:34.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:35.000
Scott & Krista: As an interim step.

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:42.000
Scott & Krista: And the call to action, I think I definitely get behind that. And I think it's good. I just don't know what the right answer is.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:58.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm. Okay. Yeah, so I think it's twofold, right? Because we do want people to respond to the text messages, but we also want people to respond or to open the emails, right? Because there's a lot of valuable information in there.

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:56.000
Scott & Krista: Right.

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:14.000
Whitney Hodges: You know links to be able to schedule a tour. There's more about the about us. We can include that that virtual tour in there as well. So I would love for the text message to do 2 jobs right to point people to their inbox so that they're finding the email and actually.

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:05.000
Scott & Krista: Yeah.

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:29.000
Whitney Hodges: opening it, um, but then also, like, asking them a question to try to get them to kind of respond to you, sort of thing. And if you really do like the first step, and I think that it's great of, uh, you know, getting on.

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:22.000
Scott & Krista: And…

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:39.000
Whitney Hodges: a call with them for… on Google Meet, or, you know, FaceTime, or something like that. I… I love that. Um, so I wonder if we say… I wonder if we…

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:36.000
Scott & Krista: Yeah.

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:43.000
Whitney Hodges: We're definitely gonna remove the pictures from the first email.

00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:57.000
Whitney Hodges: I think we could include the link to the YouTube, um, virtual tour in that first email instead. So, what if we say something in the text message, like, we've just sent an email your way with our pricing brochure, um…

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:50.000
Scott & Krista: Uh-huh.

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:04.000
Whitney Hodges: It also includes a link to our virtual website.

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:11.000
Whitney Hodges: tour, so you'll definitely want to…

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:17.000
Whitney Hodges: Check that out, and then ending with something like…

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:33.000
Whitney Hodges: is there a good time for us to hop on a quick call together? And we could kind of keep that ambiguous, because it's like a phone… I mean, a phone call with some people could be valuable, too, right? Like, after they respond, then you could say, do you want to do Google Meet? Do you want to do FaceTime? Do you want me to just call you, sort of thing?

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:21.000
Scott & Krista: Yeah.

00:26:25.000 --> 00:26:26.000
Scott & Krista: Yeah.

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:34.000
Scott & Krista: No.

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:37.000
Whitney Hodges: But I'd love to try this and see if that gets us some responses.

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:35.000
Scott & Krista: Yes.

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:43.000
Scott & Krista: I… I love that. My only… my only question about a possible tweak is…

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:51.000
Scott & Krista: Language-wise, with the last sentence, is it better to say, is there a good time? Because that could be just a yes or no.

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:48.000
Whitney Hodges: Hmm?

00:26:52.000 --> 00:27:00.000
Scott & Krista: Or maybe, like, is it better to say when is a good time to call, or does that feel too pushy?

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:53.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:58.000
Whitney Hodges: When's a good time? Sure.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:05.000
Scott & Krista: Because when requires them to answer.

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:04.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:07.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:10.000
Scott & Krista: one feels like…

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:10.000
Whitney Hodges: Right.

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:16.000
Scott & Krista: more of a call to action and response than is there?

00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:15.000
Whitney Hodges: Mmhm.

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:25.000
Scott & Krista: I don't know, but I want your input there as to… because conversely, when is a good time might feel a little pushy.

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:18.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:22.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:31.000
Whitney Hodges: Right, yeah. And it may… it could even be something like, um…

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:38.000
Whitney Hodges: Would you like to hop on a quick call together soon? If so, let me know what may be a good time.

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:35.000
Scott & Krista: Yeah.

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:45.000
Scott & Krista: I like that. Would you like to? That sounds good. Yeah, that sounds… that feels like it splits the conversational vibe a little bit, that tone.

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:41.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay.

00:27:57.000 --> 00:28:00.000
Scott & Krista: And one of the things I struggle with.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:03.000
Scott & Krista: Just as an aside, is…

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:05.000
Whitney Hodges: Yes.

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:08.000
Scott & Krista: You know, I'm…

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:20.000
Scott & Krista: I'm, like, grandpa age over here, and my coordinators who were responding to these.

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:27.000
Scott & Krista: inquiries are my kids age and like one of the things that drives me nuts.

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:25.000
Whitney Hodges: Right.

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:32.000
Scott & Krista: Is that like Valerie, who I love.

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:39.000
Scott & Krista: is a great employee, but when she responds, it's an exclamation point at the end of every sentence.

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:53.000
Scott & Krista: And I've tried to get her to express herself in a different way and to understand that if you use an exclamation point at the end of every sentence, then it loses.

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:41.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:48.000
Whitney Hodges: Mmhm.

00:28:53.000 --> 00:29:02.000
Scott & Krista: its impact, or… but then I'm like, is that a generational thing? Do I sound… does that… I don't know. I…

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:03.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:18.000
Scott & Krista: On the one hand, I understand it's important for them to have a different tone of communication with someone who's much closer to their age. And I see it on pores. Like the bride will can talk to Delia in a way that they won't talk to me. First of all.

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:12.000
Whitney Hodges: Right.

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:14.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:33.000
Scott & Krista: Delia is a woman who's also been married, and I'm their dad's age and a guy. I don't know. I don't want to get too lost in this conversation here. I guess what I'm saying is, I don't want to be overly critical of how my team responds, but I also.

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:23.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:26.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:30.000
Whitney Hodges: No.

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:35.000
Scott & Krista: Think.

00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:49.000
Scott & Krista: man, we only have one chance for that 1st impression in how you write back to that person. And like, am I wrong to think that all exclamation points is silly and kind of a turn off.

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:43.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:29:49.000 --> 00:30:06.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, I hear what you're saying. I think it's probably I mean, it may be important to kind of differentiate. And you could share this with your team, too. The difference between the Ridgefield brand voice.

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:24.000
Whitney Hodges: and an individual team member's voice, right? And it may be up to you to kind of define with them of, like, okay, you know, if you're just text messaging back and forth with, uh, you know, a potential client who hasn't come in for a tour before, maybe we need to keep more of the Ridgefield.

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:49.000
Whitney Hodges: brand voice right and kind of tone it down versus if you really do want. You know Valerie's kind of personality to shine from the beginning, I think, as long as she's making it very clear that she is text messaging them as Valerie, the coordinator, right? That that maybe has a bit of a different vibe of like, you know, if she's if she's upfront saying, Hey, this is Valerie, and like she's just enthusiastic, and that's just her.

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:44.000
Scott & Krista: That's a good point.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:49.000
Scott & Krista: Right.

00:30:49.000 --> 00:31:03.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, I don't know. Fallon may be able to speak to this a little bit, too. She's… she's a current bride-to-be. She's getting married in October. She's also a little… she's younger than I am. I'm a millennial, she's Gen Z, so what are your thoughts.

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:25.000
Fallon Looney: Yeah, I think I I don't even know. I think I'm like technically a zillennial. I just turned 30 in April. So I think I'm like the final like millennial age, where I'm like a little bit in between both, and in my opinion I don't think it necessarily makes a huge difference in terms of like.

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:11.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:31:11.000 --> 00:31:13.000
Whitney Hodges: Got it.

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:42.000
Fallon Looney: punctuation, but I do agree that where they're at in the journey makes a little bit of a difference. I find that, like, if I'm just inquiring for the very first time, I think having a bit of a, like, professional tone and, like, lay of the land is gonna.

00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:53.000
Fallon Looney: bode well, because at that point, I'm looking for, like, logistics. But then once I've engaged beyond that, and, like, booked a tour, if it's like, okay.

00:31:53.000 --> 00:32:04.000
Fallon Looney: here's the you know. I'm just using this as an example. It's not necessarily part of what we have here, but like thinking about my own experience like.

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:21.000
Fallon Looney: I responded well when things felt more like an organic conversation leading up to the tour, or, like, after the tour, where, like, some of that, you know, individualized personality with the team, um, can come out, but I think, like.

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:38.000
Fallon Looney: in the beginning, where there's still a little bit information gathering and like not as like intertwined with the communications, maybe like the 1st like 2 touch points feeling a bit more.

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:39.000
